          "guid": "ms2x05om5sr0cj1k",
          "message": {
            "text": "With per-region timing (advances), a state's overlay anchors resolve in the capture ROUND that state was driven in — which is exact for an anchor inside the region the state advances, and approximate for one inside a different region.\n\nThe assumption this relies on is the run's own checked precondition: states sharing a round advance disjoint regions, and the non-region remainder of every round must be byte-identical (the run hard-errors otherwise). So the page at round r holds this state's own region at this state's content, and the unchanging chrome everywhere else. An anchor into either is the layout the author meant.\n\nWhat is NOT exact: anchoring to an element inside a different declared region, which may be ahead of or behind this state on its own schedule. That resolves against whatever position that region holds in this round. Documented as the one caveat in docs/104-overlay-windows.md §3 with the guidance to anchor within the state's own region when placement must be exact.\n\nRound 0 is special-cased (r === 0 ? [0] : …) because plan.rounds[0] is always empty by construction — round 0 IS state 0, the frame's own post-actions state and every region's starting point — so iterating rounds[0] would silently skip state 0's overlays.\n",
            "markdown": "With per-region timing (`advances`), a state's overlay anchors resolve in the capture ROUND that state was driven in — which is exact for an anchor inside the region the state advances, and approximate for one inside a different region.\n\nThe assumption this relies on is the run's own checked precondition: states sharing a round advance *disjoint* regions, and the non-region remainder of every round must be byte-identical (the run hard-errors otherwise). So the page at round `r` holds this state's own region at this state's content, and the unchanging chrome everywhere else. An anchor into either is the layout the author meant.\n\nWhat is NOT exact: anchoring to an element inside a *different* declared region, which may be ahead of or behind this state on its own schedule. That resolves against whatever position that region holds in this round. Documented as the one caveat in `docs/104-overlay-windows.md` §3 with the guidance to anchor within the state's own region when placement must be exact.\n\nRound 0 is special-cased (`r === 0 ? [0] : …`) because `plan.rounds[0]` is always empty by construction — round 0 IS state 0, the frame's own post-`actions` state and every region's starting point — so iterating `rounds[0]` would silently skip state 0's overlays.\n"
          },
          "locations": [
