          "guid": "ms2x0tshcu9gkqz7",
          "message": {
            "text": "Removing overlays from the exclusion set changed two committed goldens; both were verified pixel-identical to their previous output before the goldens were regenerated.\n\nThis is the risky edit of the change: autoCompress is default-ON, so dropping a blocker silently alters the output shape of any config that has an overlay-carrying continue + cut run — including the flagship editor-session example, whose five typed-line frames now collapse into one run.\n\nRather than trust the compressor's pixel-identity contract, both affected examples were rasterized old-vs-new across their whole timelines and diffed with the project's own comparator (compare-pngs.ts, pass = regionCount === 0): 32 samples over form-fill's 8.2 s and 48 over editor-session's 17.2 s, every one at regionCount 0 / 0.000% non-AA, at 12% and 15% fewer bytes. Attached artifact has the full numbers and the reasoning for which frames stayed siblings.\n\nThe subtle case that could have broken and didn't: editor-session's typing overlays set holdToFrameEnd: true, whose whole point is a hard cut at the frame boundary handing off to the next frame's identical real page text. Inside the run that boundary is now the overlay's window end — which is its state's end, the same instant — so the handoff survives.\n",
            "markdown": "Removing `overlays` from the exclusion set changed two committed goldens; both were verified **pixel-identical** to their previous output before the goldens were regenerated.\n\nThis is the risky edit of the change: `autoCompress` is default-ON, so dropping a blocker silently alters the output shape of any config that has an overlay-carrying `continue` + `cut` run — including the flagship `editor-session` example, whose five typed-line frames now collapse into one run.\n\nRather than trust the compressor's pixel-identity contract, both affected examples were rasterized old-vs-new across their whole timelines and diffed with the project's own comparator (`compare-pngs.ts`, pass = `regionCount === 0`): 32 samples over form-fill's 8.2 s and 48 over editor-session's 17.2 s, **every one at regionCount 0 / 0.000% non-AA**, at 12% and 15% fewer bytes. Attached artifact has the full numbers and the reasoning for which frames stayed siblings.\n\nThe subtle case that could have broken and didn't: editor-session's typing overlays set `holdToFrameEnd: true`, whose whole point is a hard cut at the frame boundary handing off to the next frame's identical real page text. Inside the run that boundary is now the overlay's *window* end — which is its state's end, the same instant — so the handoff survives.\n"
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "src/cli/animate.ts"
                },
                "region": {
                  "startLine": 2586,
                  "endLine": 2600,
                  "snippet": {
                    "text": "    // DM-1767: each member's `overlays` become its STATE's overlays. That is\n    // what preserves the authored behavior through the collapse: the state's\n    // capture is where its anchors resolve, and the state's slice of the run is\n    // the window `buildStatesRunContent` bounds them to — so an overlay on the\n    // third of five members still dies at that member's cut instead of holding\n    // to the end of the whole run.\n    const states: RunStateInput[] = [\n      {\n        duration: runAnchor.duration, // state 0 = the anchor's own post-actions capture\n        ...(runAnchor.overlays != null && runAnchor.overlays.length > 0 ? { overlays: runAnchor.overlays } : {}),\n      },\n      ...runFrames.slice(1).map((f) => ({\n        ...(f.actions != null ? { actions: f.actions } : {}),\n        duration: f.duration,\n        ...(f.overlays != null && f.overlays.length > 0 ? { overlays: f.overlays } : {}),"
                  }
                }
              }
            }
          ],
